    81	---
    82	
    83	## 1. PCC: exact statement
    84	
    85	Let `k` be an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero.  Let
    86	
    87	\[
    88	(f,g):\mathbb A_k^2\longrightarrow\mathbb A_k^2
    89	\]
    90	
    91	be a dominant, nonautomorphic, Sigray-normalized polynomial Keller pair,
    92	so `J(f,g) in k*`, and put
    93	
    94	\[
    95	\operatorname{td}:=[k(x,y):k(f,g)].
    96	\]
    97	
    98	Write its coprime type and degrees as
    99	
   100	\[
   101	2\le\alpha<\beta,\qquad (\alpha,\beta)=1,
   102	\qquad d=\deg f=B\alpha,\quad e=\deg g=B\beta,
   103	\]
   104	
   105	where `B=gcd(d,e)`.  Let
   106	
   107	\[
   108	F(X,Y,Z)=Z^df(X/Z,Y/Z),\qquad
   109	G(X,Y,Z)=Z^eg(X/Z,Y/Z)
   110	\]
   111	
   112	and consider the two pencils
   113	
   114	\[
   115	\mathcal L_f=\langle F,Z^d\rangle,
   116	\qquad \mathcal L_g=\langle G,Z^e\rangle,
   117	\]
   118	
   119	or equivalently the rational maps `[F:Z^d]` and `[G:Z^e]` from
   120	`P^2` to `P^1`.
   121	
   122	Let
   123	
   124	\[
   125	\pi:X\longrightarrow\mathbb P^2
   126	\]
   127	
   128	be the minimal simultaneous resolution by point blowups on which both
   129	pencil maps are morphisms.  Let `K_f` and `K_g` be their clusters of proper
   130	and infinitely-near base centers.  For every
   131	`p in K_f union K_g`, define:
   132	
   133	- `R_p` as the order at `p`, immediately before `p` is blown up, of the
   134	  mobile strict transform of a general member of `L_f`;
   135	- `S_p` analogously for `L_g`;
   136	- the value to be zero for a pencil for which `p` is not a base center.
   137	
   138	Thus `R_p,S_p` are the point-basis coefficients of the two generic fiber
   139	classes, not multiplicities of a selected special member and not
   140	coefficients in the strict-exceptional basis.  Define the coordinatewise
   141	integral meet
   142	
   143	\[
   144	h_p:=\min\!\left(
   145	\left\lfloor\frac{R_p}{\alpha}\right\rfloor,
   146	\left\lfloor\frac{S_p}{\beta}\right\rfloor
   147	\right).\tag{1.1}
   148	\]
   149	
   150	> **CONJECTURE PCC (proportional-center coverage).** For every pair above,
   151	> on its minimal simultaneous base cluster,
   152	> \[
   153	> \sum_{p\in K_f\cup K_g}h_p^2\ge B^2-1.\tag{1.2}
   154	> \]
   155	
   156	In the orthogonal total-transform point basis, put
   157	
   158	\[
   159	H_{\cap}:=\sum_ph_pE_p^*.
   160	\]
   161	
   162	Then PCC is equivalently
   163	
   164	\[
   165	-H_{\cap}^2\ge B^2-1.\tag{1.3}
   166	\]
   167	
   168	This notation does **not** assert that `(h_p)` satisfies proximity
   169	inequalities, is antinef, or is itself the base cluster of a complete
   170	ideal.  It is only a coordinatewise meet in the orthogonal point basis.
   171	Using the minimal simultaneous cluster removes resolution-choice
   172	ambiguity.  A further fixed point blowup after both pencils are resolved
   173	adds zero generic multiplicities, so a legitimate common refinement does
   174	not change (1.2).
   175	
   176	### 1.1 PCC is sufficient
   177	
   178	Let `H` be the pullback of a line and use the orthogonal total-transform
   179	basis.  The generic fiber classes are
   180	
   181	\[
   182	C=B\alpha H-\sum_pR_pE_p^*,\qquad
   183	D=B\beta H-\sum_pS_pE_p^* .\tag{1.4}
   184	\]
   185	
   186	The resolved pencils give `C^2=D^2=0`.  For a generic pair of finite target
   187	values, the boundary divisor avoids that target point, while the affine
   188	intersection has degree `td`; hence `C.D=td`.  Therefore
   189	
   190	\[
   191	\sum_pR_p^2=B^2\alpha^2,\qquad
   192	\sum_pS_p^2=B^2\beta^2,\qquad
   193	\sum_pR_pS_p=B^2\alpha\beta-\operatorname{td}.\tag{1.5}
   194	\]
   195	
   196	By (1.1), term by term,
   197	
   198	\[
   199	R_p\ge\alpha h_p,\qquad S_p\ge\beta h_p,\qquad
   200	R_pS_p\ge\alpha\beta h_p^2.\tag{1.6}
   201	\]
   202	
   203	Consequently PCC gives
   204	
   205	\[
   206	\begin{aligned}
   207	B^2\alpha\beta-\operatorname{td}
   208	 &=\sum_pR_pS_p\\
   209	 &\ge\alpha\beta\sum_ph_p^2\\
   210	 &\ge\alpha\beta(B^2-1),
   211	\end{aligned}
   212	\]
   213	
   214	and hence
   215	
   216	\[
   217	\boxed{\operatorname{td}\le\alpha\beta}.\tag{1.7}
   218	\]
   219	
   220	This is the exact sufficiency argument audited in
   221	`xmodel/grok-k-g5-review.md:525-533,579-583`, strengthening the initial
   222	formulation at `xmodel/sol-g5-emission.md:1014-1048` by fixing the cluster
   223	and point-basis conventions.
   224	
   225	There is also an exact mismatch identity.  With
   226	
   227	\[
   228	\Delta:=C/\alpha-D/\beta,
   229	\]
   230	
   231	(1.5) gives
   232	
   233	\[
   234	\sum_p\left(\frac{R_p}{\alpha}-\frac{S_p}{\beta}\right)^2
   235	=\frac{2\operatorname{td}}{\alpha\beta},
   236	\qquad
   237	\Delta^2=-\frac{2\operatorname{td}}{\alpha\beta}.\tag{1.8}
   238	\]
   239	
   240	Thus KME-2, `Delta^2 >= -2`, is exactly the desired TD bound rewritten.
   241	It is not an independent sufficient mechanism.  PCC is formally stronger
   242	as a proposed point-basis condition: it termwise implies the bound, while
   243	no converse is asserted or banked.  It is the only named target in the G5
   244	audit that could genuinely imply the sharp bound without simply renaming
   245	it.
   246	
   247	### 1.2 Equality exposes PCC's strength
   248	
   249	If `td=alpha*beta` and PCC holds, both summed inequalities in (1.7), and
   250	the product inequality `R_p*S_p >= alpha*beta*h_p^2` at every center, are
   251	equalities.  Therefore
   252	
   253	\[
   254	\sum_ph_p^2=B^2-1,
   255	\]
   256	
   257	and, for every center with `h_p>0`,
   258	
   259	\[
   260	R_p=\alpha h_p,\qquad S_p=\beta h_p.
   261	\]
   262	
   263	Every center with `h_p=0` must have `R_pS_p=0`.  Thus a sharp pair would
   264	have no residual shared multiplicity away from exactly proportional
   265	centers.  PCC is correspondingly strong; the calculation proves its
   266	sufficiency, not its truth.
   267	
   268	---
   269	
   270	## 2. PCC proof attempt from the bank
   271	
   272	### 2.1 The one available local foothold
   273	
   274	The common-power conclusion of GGV Corollary 7.4, in a certified
   275	Laurent/weighted frame and specialized to the type ratio, has the form
   276	
   277	\[
   278	\ell(P)=\lambda\widetilde R^{q\alpha},\qquad
   279	\ell(Q)=\mu\widetilde R^{q\beta}.\tag{2.1}
   280	\]
   281	
   282	More generally a factor of ordinary order `t` in `R_tilde` occurs to
   283	orders `q*alpha*t` and `q*beta*t`.  This gives the following elementary
   284	conditional statement.
   285	
   286	> **Conditional local lemma.** Suppose a theorem transports (2.1) together
   287	> with the transformed denominator sections `Z^d,Z^e` to a named center
   288	> `p` of the simultaneous projective base cluster.  After removing the
   289	> recorded fixed exceptional factors, suppose the *mobile strict transforms
   290	> of generic pencil members* `F-cZ^d` and `G-c'Z^e` have lowest ordinary
   291	> local forms `lambda_0*R_0^(q*alpha)` and
   292	> `mu_0*R_0^(q*beta)`, where `ord_p(R_0)=t`.  Then
   293	> \[
   294	> R_p\ge q\alpha t,\qquad S_p\ge q\beta t,
   295	> \qquad h_p\ge qt.\tag{2.2}
   296	> \]
   297	
   298	Once the hypothesis is available, (2.2) is immediate.  The hypothesis is
   299	not banked.  `SECTION4-AUTOMATION.md:140-163` certifies the edge power;
   300	`xmodel/sol-tdbound-review.md:437-483` records precisely why it does not yet
   301	identify an ordinary base-center multiplicity.
   302	
   303	Weighted initials alone cannot make that identification.  For example,
   304	
   305	```text
   306	u^(q*alpha) + x,       u^(q*beta) + 2*x
   307	```
   308	
   309	can display the desired powers on a selected weighted face while ordinary
   310	multiplicity and intersection are controlled by the lower `x` terms.  One
   311	must carry the actual chart substitution, exceptional monomial, ordinary
   312	maximal ideal, and proximity ancestry.
   313	
   314	### 2.2 The minimal obstruction in the common-power lane
   315	
   316	The first absent theorem can be stated without asking for all of PCC.
   317	
   318	> **CONJECTURE WTC-1 (single-packet weighted-to-center transport).** Let a
   319	> certified Corollary 7.4 cut contain a named factor packet
   320	> `R_tilde^(q*alpha*t), R_tilde^(q*beta*t)`.  Transport the complete base
   321	> ideals `(F,Z^d)` and `(G,Z^e)`, including both denominator sections, by an
   322	> explicitly constructed chart map into the minimal simultaneous point
   323	> resolution.  It produces a named proper or infinitely-near center `p`
   324	> such that, after stripping the recorded exceptional factors, the generic
   325	> mobile multiplicities obey (2.2).  The construction records the immediate
   326	> proximity parents of `p` and is functorial under the next Laurent cut.
   327	
   328	This is the **minimal sub-obstruction in the Corollary-7.4 packet-to-PCC
   329	lane**: the bank cannot prove even the one-packet arrow before any summation
   330	is attempted.  A different global route, such as log ramification, could
   331	bypass this arrow and prove the TD bound directly.
   332	
   333	If WTC-1 is proved, a second statement is still required.
   334	
   335	> **CONJECTURE PCC-COVER (coverage and no double counting).** The packet
   336	> centers produced along all cuts can be identified or separated according
   337	> to their actual proximity ancestry so that integers `c_p <= h_p` satisfy
   338	> \[
   339	> \sum_pc_p^2\ge B^2-1.\tag{2.3}
   340	> \]
   341	
   342	Repeated visibility of one factor cannot be summed as if it supplied new
   343	centers.  Conversely, successive transforms of a factor can contribute at
   344	successive infinitely-near centers only after the blowup charts prove that
   345	ancestry.  In point-basis language the target is the one-unit residual
   346	point-basis energy bound
   347	
   348	\[
   349	B^2-\sum_ph_p^2\le1.\tag{2.4}
   350	\]
   351	
   352	None of the current inputs bounds this residual.
   353	
   354	### 2.3 Why FC5 stops before WTC-1
   355	
   356	The exact FC5 emission is
   357	
   358	\[
   359	w_G=\frac{\bar\kappa_G(d_q-1)}{\nu_Gd_q},\qquad
   360	M_G=\gcd(d_p,d_q).\tag{2.5}
   361	\]
   362	
   363	At a pole,
   364	
   365	\[
   366	\Phi_s(w,M):=\frac{wM^2}{sM-1}=\frac{ab}{\nu},
   367	\qquad
   368	\frac{\operatorname{td}}{\alpha\beta}
   369	=\sum_{P\text{ pole}}\Phi_s(w_P,M_P).\tag{2.6}
   370	\]
   371	
   372	Equations (2.5)-(2.6) are exact, but FC5 consumes a local frame and emits
   373	only two scalars.  It contains no incoming pole mass, root position,
   374	ordinary center, exceptional divisor, proximity relation, or cross-chart
   375	identifier.  Moreover `Phi_s` is geometric only at poles and is
   376	nonmonotone on internal coefficient-solvable charts in both directions.
   377	Thus FC5 identifies the scalar quantity that the global geometry must
   378	control, but cannot place any contribution into `(R_p,S_p)`.
   379	
   380	Source: `xmodel/sol-g5-emission.md:69-206,306-378` and the hostile audit at
   381	`xmodel/grok-k-g5-review.md:375-494`.
   382	
   383	### 2.4 Why the `w` depth invariant stops before WTC-1
   384	
   385	On an `M=1` chain the bank proves
   386	
   387	\[
   388	w_{j+1}=w_j\frac{n}{(n-1)\nu+1}.\tag{2.7}
   389	\]
   390	
   391	Neutral steps preserve `w`; resonant steps contract it, so the cumulative
   392	jump-cell menu is finite for a **fixed entry**.  This is useful depth
   393	closure, but:
   394	
   395	- the Puiseux denominator and endpoint frame are not bounded;
   396	- the theorem does not bound distinct entries across the TD ladder;
   397	- its printed scope excludes `M>=2` suffixes; and
   398	- `w` has no banked point-cluster or proximity interpretation.
   399	
   400	It therefore cannot construct the center in WTC-1.  See
   401	`SHEET6-DEPTH.md:28-51,128-138,142-205,403-431`.
   402	
   403	### 2.5 Why NF-M's Bezout theorem stops before WTC-1
   404	
   405	For a **fixed** discrete `nu>=2` merge schema, NF-M reduces the local Fuchs
   406	equation to `Qhat-1` homogeneous equations in `Qhat-1` essential orbit
   407	variables.  The equation of index `j` has degree `Qhat-j`, giving the
   408	projective Bezout bound
   409	
   410	\[
   411	(\widehat Q-1)!\tag{2.8}
   412	\]
   413	
   414	on zero-dimensional coefficient types modulo permutation and scaling.
   415	Positive-dimensional components are retained, and future locality requires
   416	the same type **and the same emitted frame**.
   417	
   418	This Bezout theorem counts local coefficient decorations after a schema and
   419	emitted frame have already been fixed.  It does not count base centers,
   420	identify `I_infinity`, or produce a point-basis vector.  In particular, it
   421	supplies no uniform bound while those discrete inputs—and hence the
   422	`B`-related degree data—vary.  NF-M can certify a factor `R_tilde` after a
   423	center dictionary exists; it cannot create that dictionary.  See
   424	`NF-M.md:31-129,257-272`.
   425	
   426	### 2.6 Exact scale-loss control
   427	
   428	For
   429	
   430	\[
   431	A=30t+5,
   432	\]
   433	
   434	the bank contains a formal type-`(2,3)` one-pole arithmetic schema with
   435	
   436	\[
   437	(a,b,\nu)=(A,2,3),\qquad
   438	\operatorname{td}=4A,\qquad \frac{ab}{\nu}=\frac{2A}{3},\tag{2.9}
   439	\]
   440	
   441	but a locally admissible IIa step emits the constant state
   442	
   443	\[
   444	(w,M)=(2/3,3).\tag{2.10}
   445	\]
   446	
   447	The child has `Qhat=2` and one NF-M type for every `A`.  The parent and
   448	child coefficient symbols are intentionally independent because the
   449	missing parent-to-child substitution/gluing is not banked.
   450	
   451	This is **not** a Keller counterexample and is not claimed globally
   452	realizable.  It is an exact quantifier control: FC5 plus the displayed
   453	local arithmetic, local ODEs, depth closure, and finite NF-M type count do
   454	not imply PCC, SP, or even a finite type-`(2,3)` bound.  A proof must use
   455	the omitted cross-chart gluing and global base-cluster information.  See
   456	`xmodel/sol-g5-emission.md:387-520,621-676` and
   457	`xmodel/grok-k-g5-review.md:432-496`.
   458	
   459	### 2.7 Why the 21-of-22 census is not coverage
   460	
   461	If `q` is the number of pole vertices, the pole laws give
   462	
   463	\[
   464	\operatorname{td}=\sum_i\Lambda_i,\qquad \Lambda_i\ge\beta.
   465	\]
   466	
   467	Thus `q>alpha` violates TD-BOUND immediately.  If `q=alpha`, equality
   468	requires every pole to be beta-minimal, and MP4 then forces every `b_i=1`;
   469	an off-axis row with `q=alpha` also violates automatically.  These two
   470	selection rules force 21 of the 22 recorded violations.  The census has no
   471	`R_p,S_p`, no proximity matrix, and no realizability theorem.  Its
   472	frequency is therefore a construction effect, not evidence for (2.3).
   473	
   474	The one-pole cap `ab<=nu` is the mandatory smallest sector test for any
   475	emission proof, but it is exactly TD-BOUND in the one-pole sector.  It is
   476	not a substitute for PCC.  See `TDBOUND.md:7-20`,
   477	`xmodel/sol-g5-emission.md:884-918`, and
   478	`xmodel/sol-tdbound-review.md:211-257`.
   479	
   480	### 2.8 Ranked routes
   481	
   482	| rank | route | what it could establish | priority | difficulty |
   483	|---:|---|---|---|---:|
   484	| 1 | Complete ideals and proximity | Principalize `(F,Z^d)` and `(G,Z^e)`, transport each Corollary 7.4 factor through explicit toric/blowup charts, compute the point-basis/proximity matrix, and prove residual energy (2.4). This attacks PCC directly and enforces no-double-counting naturally. | HIGH | 8.5/10 |
   485	| 2 | Log ramification/Jacobian divisor | On `Phi:X -> P1 x P1`, use `K_X-Phi^*K` and boundary ramification to force `Delta^2 >= -2`. This may bypass floors and PCC, but targets the TD/KME statement directly. | HIGH | 9/10 |
   486	| 3 | Exact one-pole gluing stress test | Write the actual Puiseux/chart substitution for the `A`-family in one coefficient ring, impose both local equations, and eliminate. A contradiction would kill this sharp scale-loss schema; without a completeness theorem it would not prove any broader SP sector, much less PCC. | HIGH diagnostic | 7.5/10 |
   487	| 4 | Vector-valued FC5 | Enrich the state by point-basis increments and proximity parents, then seek a telescope. Scalar `(w,M)` is proved insufficient; this route becomes meaningful only after WTC-1. | MEDIUM | 9/10 |
   488	| 5 | More NF-M or census enumeration alone | Useful for fixed-schema falsification and regression only. The one-type-for-every-`A` family prevents a uniform proof from these counts. | LOW | not a viable uniform proof |
   489	
   490	For the common-power route, the most economical next target is WTC-1 in one
   491	explicit Corollary 7.4 chart, including its point-basis and proximity
   492	output.  Trying to prove the global square sum within that route before the
   493	local dictionary exists reverses the logical dependency.
   494	
