    38	## 1. The common algorithm (what the four proofs actually do)
    39	
    40	All four proofs are instances of one loop:
    41	
    42	```
    43	load family F = (A0, A0', chain [(A1,A1'),...], k, (m,n))     # GGV5 data
    44	S := start polygon (1/m-units), P-support = m·S, Q-support = n·S
    45	repeat (over boundary sections, low side first, chain order):
    46	    1. CERTIFY an edge-power: Cor 7.4/7.2 at (ρ0,σ0)=dir(A_h−A_h') with
    47	       q = q_h from (q_k)  ⇒  ℓ_{ρ,σ}(P) = λ·R^{q·m} on the adjacent
    48	       direction interval; en(R) = (1/q)·A_h ∈ ℤ² forced.
    49	    2. LOCATE st(R): finite candidate list via Algorithm 1 /
    50	       GGV2 Prop 3.12 / GGV6 Prop 2.5; kill candidates by support-level
    51	       contradictions (vdE 10.2.6, PLLC, Thm 7.6 divisibility).
    52	    3. FACTOR-BRANCH: enumerate root-multiplicity partitions of R in
    53	       z = x^K y; kill partitions via Prop 3.12(2) (forced multiplicity ϑ)
    54	       and λ≠0 side-arguments.  ← subcase splits live HERE.
    55	    4. CUT: apply e_K(λ₁): y ↦ y + λ₁x^{−K} (an Aut(L^(1)), bracket-
    56	       preserving); the certified factor (z−λ₁)^{t₁qm} becomes z^{t₁qm}:
    57	       the edge shortens or collapses; y^d prefactors spawn a Laurent
    58	       tail (−K·d,0)-ward.  Iterate 1–4 while chain data remains.
    59	until support stabilizes (no certifiable edge with q>1 remains cuttable)
    60	5. TAIL RESOLUTION (only if a residual vertex with unknown following edge
    61	   exists): opposite-vertex divisibility filter + GGV1 Prop 8.2 ⇒
    62	   {en(P), en(Q)} = {(−k,0),(k+1,1)}, k forced (=1 in all §4 cases),
    63	   P/Q assignment by a cross-product test. Supersedes provisional tails.
    64	6. FINALIZE: ψ_j: x ↦ x^{−1}, y ↦ x^j y (L^(1)-morphism, NOT in Aut(K[x,y]))
    65	   with j = ⌈max_{(i,j')∈supp, j'>0} i/j'⌉; supports (i,j') ↦ (j·j'−i, j');
    66	   bracket: [ψP,ψQ] = −x^{j−2}[P,Q]  ⇒  RHS = x^{j−2} after scalar scaling.
    67	report (N(P), N(Q), x^{j−2}, branch label, justification log)
    68	```
    69	
    70	**Why 4.3 yields x² and the others x:** the RHS exponent is j−2 and
    71	j = ⌈b₀/a₀⌉ on the original corner A₀=(a₀,b₀) (= slope of the steep side that ψ_j
    72	must rotate into the quadrant). (9,27),(9,24),(7,21): ⌈27/9⌉=⌈24/9⌉=⌈21/7⌉=3 ⇒ x.
    73	(8,28): ⌈28/8⌉=⌈3.5⌉=4 ⇒ x². (Farm preview: (7,35)-type would give ⌈5⌉−2 ⇒ x³.)
    74	The bracket exponent is thus pure lattice data, known before any branching.
    75	
    76	**Why 4.4 has no tail-resolution stage:** its single cut collapses the edge
    77	completely (st(R)=(1,0), no y-prefactor ⇒ the transformed edge is a monomial)
    78	and the remaining boundary consists of original chain edges only — no residual
    79	vertex with unknown continuation is created. In 4.1/4.2/4.3 the second cut leaves
    80	the residual vertex (en − qm·t₁·(K,1)-type, e.g. (21,8), (24,7)) whose next edge
    81	is unknown ⇒ step 5 runs.
