   116	## 3. Residue-A: independent `I_infinity` calculation
   117	
   118	The rectangular corners in `SHEET6-TEMPLATE.md:61-64` give
   119	
   120	```text
   121	(k_f,l_f)=(126,42),  deg f=168;
   122	(k_g,l_g)=(189,63),  deg g=252;
   123	B=gcd(168,252)=84,   (alpha,beta)=(2,3).
   124	```
   125	
   126	For generic levels, the two projective points at infinity contribute as
   127	follows.
   128	
   129	```text
   130	Y=[1:0:0]:  I_Y = 126*252 + 84*(-1/14)
   131	                  = 31752 - 6 = 31746.
   132	
   133	X=[0:1:0]:  I_X = 42*252 = 10584.
   134	
   135	I_infinity = I_Y + I_X = 42330.
   136	td         = 168*252 - 42330 = 6.
   137	```
   138	
   139	The `-1/14` pole order is independently recovered from the product of the
   140	recorded branch contacts (`cases/classical_tests.py:100-107`); the complete
   141	calculation is encoded at `cases/classical_tests.py:180-189` and printed in
   142	`SHEET6-CLASSICAL.md:278-285`.  Numerically,
   143	
   144	```text
   145	I_infinity/(deg f*deg g) = 42330/42336
   146	                         = 7055/7056
   147	                         = 99.9858277...%,
   148	I_infinity = (B^2-1)*alpha*beta = (84^2-1)*6.
   149	```
   150	
   151	This is an exact consistency check **conditional on the formal Puiseux/Newton
   152	genome**.  It is not an observation from a realized polynomial Keller map.
   153	`SHEET6-TEMPLATE.md:4-12` calls the object `FORMAL-CANDIDATE`, and
   154	`BOOK-ENUM.md:149-154` explicitly leaves polynomial realizability untracked.
   155	Consequently `TDBOUND.md` should not call residue-A “the one realized record.”
