   209	## 2. Orbitwise minimality of a rectangular cusp pair
   210	
   211	### Theorem 2.1 (exact source/target degree transport)
   212	
   213	Let \(1<r<s\) be coprime.  Suppose \(P,Q\in\mathbf C[x,y]\) have, for some
   214	positive integers \(a,b\),
   215	
   216	\[
   217	\begin{aligned}
   218	 \operatorname{Supp}P&\subseteq[0,ra]\times[0,rb],
   219	 & [x^{ra}y^{rb}]P&=c\ne0,\\
   220	 \operatorname{Supp}Q&\subseteq[0,sa]\times[0,sb],
   221	 & [x^{sa}y^{sb}]Q&=d\ne0.
   222	\end{aligned}
   223	\tag{2.1}
   224	\]
   225	
   226	For a source automorphism \(L=(u,v)\), put
   227	
   228	\[
   229	 D_L=a\deg u+b\deg v,qquad H=u_+^av_+^b,
   230	\tag{2.2}
   231	\]
   232	
   233	where \(u_+,v_+\) are the top ordinary homogeneous parts.  Then, for every
   234	target coordinate \(h(U,V)\),
   235	
   236	\[
   237	 \deg h(P\circ L,Q\circ L)=D_L\deg_{r,s}h.
   238	\tag{2.3}
   239	\]
   240	
   240	
   241	If \(K=(h_1,h_2)\) is any target automorphism, then
   242	
   243	\[
   244	 \bigl(\deg h_1(P\circ L,Q\circ L),
   245	       \deg h_2(P\circ L,Q\circ L)\bigr)
   246	 \ge_{\rm lex}
   247	 \bigl(r(a+b),s(a+b)\bigr).
   248	\tag{2.4}
   249	\]
   250	
   251	Consequently, if \((P,Q)\) is a counterexample with its \(r\)-component
   252	first, it is almost normalized in Sigray's source/target equivalence class.
   253	
   254	#### Proof
   255	
   256	Every source coordinate is nonconstant, so \(\deg u,\deg v\ge1\) and
   257	
   258	\[
   259	 D_L\ge a+b.
   260	\tag{2.5}
   261	\]
   262	
   263	The northeast monomial in each rectangle is the unique term maximizing the
   264	positive degree functional
   265	\((i,j)\mapsto i\deg u+j\deg v\).  Therefore
   266	
   267	\[
   268	 (P\circ L)_+=cH^r,\qquad (Q\circ L)_+=dH^s,
   269	\tag{2.6}
   270	\]
   271	
   272	and their degrees are \(rD_L,sD_L\).  For a target polynomial \(h\), only
   273	its maximal \((r,s)\)-face can contribute in ordinary degree
   274	\(D_L\deg_{r,s}h\).  Cancellation at that degree would say
   275	
   276	\[
   277	 \operatorname{in}_{r,s}h(cZ^r,dZ^s)=0,
   278	\]
   279	
   280	which Theorem 1.1 forbids when \(h\) is a coordinate.  This proves (2.3).
   281	
   282	Every nonconstant polynomial has \((r,s)\)-degree at least \(r\), so the
   283	first degree in (2.4) is at least \(rD_L\ge r(a+b)\).  Equality throughout
   284	forces \(D_L=a+b\) and \(\deg_{r,s}h_1=r\).  Since \(r<s\), the latter
   285	identity forces
   286	
   287	\[
   288	 h_1=\gamma U+\gamma_0,\qquad \gamma\ne0.
   289	\]
   290	
   291	Now \(J(h_1,h_2)=\gamma\,\partial h_2/\partial V\in\mathbf C^*\), so
   292	\(h_2\) has a nonzero term linear in \(V\) and
   293	\(\deg_{r,s}h_2\ge s\).  Formula (2.3) gives the required second-coordinate
   294	bound.  Thus no source/target equivalent representative has a smaller
   295	ordered degree pair. \(\square\)
   296	
   297	Two points are worth isolating.
   298	
   299	1. The theorem is stronger than global GGV minimality.  It proves minimum
   300	   degree inside this pair's entire Sigray equivalence class directly.
   301	2. Ordinary nondivisibility of \(rD_L,sD_L\) alone would not be enough:
   302	   \(P^s-Q^r\) can cancel.  Theorem 1.1 is exactly what excludes such a
   303	   cancellation in a *coordinate* of a target automorphism.
