    76	## 1. Edge-power combs: the GGV chain predicts a finite Eggers–Wall menu
    77	
    78	**Gap:** G2 remainder — `TRANSPORT.md` Conjecture T; `REDUCTION.md`
    79	CRITICAL 3. T2→T4 is real; the GGV ledger still does not name a Sigray
    80	tree.  
    81	**Side:** proof. The payoff is that the sheet engine starts from a
    82	*predicted finite list of decorated combs*, not from a search over T7.
    83	
    84	### Five-line pitch
    85	
    86	1. GGV Cor. 7.4 says every certified edge is a power
    87	   \(\ell_{\rho,\sigma}(P)=\lambda R^{qm}\). A plane branch (or a finite
    88	   cluster of them) whose Newton faces are all powers has embedded
    89	   resolution a *comb*: a spine indexed by the chain, with teeth the
    90	   distinct roots of each \(R_h\).
    91	2. The lattice data already in `lib/families.py` bound the comb: the
    92	   direction list is the spine, each \(q_h\) is the contact of that
    93	   tooth-generation, and the number of teeth at step \(h\) is at most
    94	   \(\deg R_h\), which is a face-length quotient and is finite.
    95	3. Pole status is not a further jet: it is whether the companion
    96	   leading form of \(Q\) *vanishes* at that root of \(R_h\). Shared root
    97	   ⇒ residual cancellation (possibly not a pole). Residual nonvanishing
    98	   ⇒ the place is a pole and the mass is an intersection number of the
    99	   two leading forms.
   100	4. So the functor is not “chain → unique tree.” It is “chain → finite
   101	   set of decorated combs,” one per root-sharing pattern. That set is
   102	   the missing G2 input to T6–T7.
   103	5. Combined with the already-proved rotations of `TRANSPORT.md`, the
   104	   T2-selected pair lands in a *computed* T7 menu. The book then
   105	   adjudicates a list, not a fork.
   106	
   107	### Nearest neighbor, and why this is not it
   108	
   109	Sol's Rank 3 (`xmodel/sol-lateral3.md`) is a paired, fiber-tagged
   110	*Newton–Puiseux cut forest* — a jet construction that carries every
   111	residual coefficient. This idea never opens a Puiseux jet. Yesterday's
   112	#2 pushes directions by \(\mathrm{SL}_2\) and reads a type; yesterday's
   113	#3 identifies GGV6 pairings with masses. Neither produces a tree
   114	*shape*. Favre–Jonsson is a dynamical eigenvaluation on the valuative
   115	tree and was already declined. Approximate-root *ladders* are the tower
   116	trust set; they have never been read as the dual graph of a comb.
   117	
   118	### First concrete test
   119	
   120	Work on the polynomial start polygons, before \(\psi_j\).
   121	
   122	1. For the four gate-B chains in `lib/FAMILIES.md` —
   123	   \((9,27)\), \((9,24)\), \((8,28)\), \((7,21)\), directions
   124	   \(((1,0),(3,-1))\), \(((3,-1))\), \(((4,-1))\), \(((7,-2))\) and
   125	   tags \((p_h,q_h)\) as in `cd.steps` — compute, from face lengths and
   126	   \(q_h\) alone, the maximum number of teeth at each spine vertex.
   127	   Emit the finite set of abstract combs.
   128	2. Decorate each comb by the \(2^{\#\text{teeth}}\) root-sharing
   129	   patterns (Q-leading-form vanishes or not). Discard any decoration
   130	   that makes every place finite: a Keller pair of type \((\alpha,\beta)\)
   131	   with \(td\ge 6\) has at least one pole.
   132	3. Controls, with coefficients: \((x,y+x^k)\) and \((x+y^2,y)\), put in
   133	   GGV-standard shape if they admit one, otherwise recorded as
   134	   “no-chain, td=1.” Their actual Eggers–Wall trees (hand, one page)
   135	   must lie in the predicted menu, and every place of \(g\) must be
   136	   finite. A predicted pole on an automorphism is a bug in the sharing
   137	   rule, not a JC2 theorem.
   138	4. One farm-side check: the \((8,28)\) comb menu, pushed through
   139	   `TRANSPORT.md` (4.1)–(4.5), must not contain a T7 entry that the
   140	   Bézout residual of that support forbids. A forbidden output is a
   141	   dictionary bug.
   142	5. No `msolve`. Output: four finite lists of decorated combs, plus
   143	   two automorphism controls.
   144	
   145	### Kill criterion
   146	
   147	Kill if the predicted menu, on any of the four chains, is *all* of T7
   148	at every \(td\) compatible with the support — then the comb restriction
   149	is vacuous and G2 has not been reduced. Kill if a realized
   150	automorphism's tree is not a comb of this type (a genuine branch point
   151	that is not a Cor. 7.4 tooth). Kill if two coherent polynomial pairs
   152	with the same chain and the same leading-form root-sharing have
   153	different labelled pole trees: then jets really are necessary and the
   154	thin functor is insufficient. A menu that works on automorphisms and is
   155	empty on gate B is information (those chains cannot be realized as
   156	Keller pairs in this decoration language), not a kill.
