   160	## 2. GGV-fan exceptional orders: pole status is a support function
   161	
   162	**Gap:** the same G2 remainder, from the dual side.  
   163	**Side:** proof. Eggers–Wall *is* the dual graph of the embedded
   164	resolution; this writes that graph from the GGV fan.
   165	
   166	### Five-line pitch
   167	
   168	1. A GGV complete chain is a finite list of directions \((\rho_h,\sigma_h)\).
   169	   Those rays, plus the two axes, are a fan \(\Sigma\) in \(N_{\mathbf R}\).
   170	   The toric blowup of the line at infinity along \(\Sigma\) is a finite
   171	   computation from `cd.steps`.
   172	2. On each exceptional \(E_h\), \(\operatorname{ord}_{E_h}(P)\) and
   173	   \(\operatorname{ord}_{E_h}(Q)\) are the support functions of the
   174	   polygons \(mS\) and \(nS\). No coefficients enter.
   175	3. After Cor. 7.4, each edge leading form factors. The non-toric
   176	   blowups that finish the embedded resolution sit at the intersection
   177	   points of the proper transform with \(E_h\), one per distinct root,
   178	   and are again finite.
   179	4. A component is a *pole* of a generic \(f\)-fiber precisely when
   180	   \(\operatorname{ord}_E(Q)\) is strictly smaller than the order
   181	   forced by the type ratio — i.e. when \(Q\) is more polar than
   182	   \(P^{n/m}\) along \(E\). Residual cancellation is the opposite
   183	   inequality. That is Conjecture T's missing bit, as a lattice
   184	   inequality on exceptional divisors.
   185	5. The dual graph of this (toric + tooth) configuration, decorated by
   186	   those orders, *is* the Sigray Eggers–Wall pole tree of the
   187	   T2-selected pair, up to the already-proved rotations.
   188	
   189	### Nearest neighbor, and why this is not it
   190	
   191	This is the resolution dual of idea 1 and of Sol's Newton-cut functor.
   192	It is not Favre–Jonsson: there is no eigenvaluation, no tautological
   193	metric, no dynamics. It is not yesterday's \(\mathrm{SL}_2\)-push: the
   194	fan is not pushed into a Sigray chamber and then abandoned; the
   195	exceptional *orders* are the decorations. FACE-ISOLATION uses mixed
   196	volume as a char-\(p\) slogan, not as an order along \(E_h\).
   197	`SHEET6-CLASSICAL.md` T1 computes splice determinants *after* the sheet
   198	data are chosen; this computes the dual graph *from the GGV fan*,
   199	before any sheet packet.
   200	
   201	### First concrete test
   202	
   203	1. Build \(\Sigma\) for each of the four gate-B direction lists. Write
   204	   the support functions of \(mS\) and \(nS\) on every ray (the
   205	   polygons are in `supports(cd)`).
   206	2. For each ray, record the sign of
   207	   \(n\cdot\operatorname{ord}_E(P)-m\cdot\operatorname{ord}_E(Q)\).
   208	   This is the candidate pole bit.
   209	3. Same two automorphism controls as idea 1: every exceptional order
   210	   of \(g\) on a generic \(f\)-fiber must come out nonpolar. One
   211	   monomial-map control: the rectangular hull of residue-A,
   212	   \((k_f,l_f)=(126,42)\), \((k_g,l_g)=(189,63)\), must produce
   213	   *exactly two* polar components, matching \(\Lambda=(3,3)\). That
   214	   hull is not residue-A, but it is the leading-form shadow, and the
   215	   order computation is exact on it.
   216	4. Compare the dual graph of the \((8,28)\) fan, after the
   217	   `TRANSPORT.md` axis swap, with the comb menu of idea 1 on the same
   218	   chain. Disagreement is a bug in one of the two dictionaries.
   219	5. No Groebner. Output: four fans, a table of orders, a pole-bit
   220	   vector, one residue-A-shadow control.
   221	
   222	### Kill criterion
   223	
   224	Kill if the pole bit along a ray depends on the choice of linear
   225	coordinates inside the GGV-standard class (it must be invariant under
   226	the residual torus and under the already-proved \(C,R\)). Kill if the
   227	residue-A rectangular shadow produces a number of polar components
   228	other than two, *and* the missing or extra components cannot be
   229	accounted for as non-toric teeth of the \((2,3)\) leading form. Kill if
   230	the dual graph changes when the target automorphism \((f,g)\mapsto(f,g+f^2)\)
   231	is applied: target mixing must not move source exceptional orders of
   232	the *pair* except by an \(\mathrm{SL}_2^{\mathrm{target}}\) action that
   233	the decoration already tracks. A fan that is too coarse (only toric,
   234	no teeth) is a failed first approximation, not a kill; add the
   235	Cor. 7.4 teeth and rerun.
