   239	## 3. Higman–Dickson landing: live off-axis records are well-quasi-ordered
   240	
   241	**Gap:** G4 — `REDUCTION.md` CRITICAL 5; `BOOK-OFFAXIS.md` P5. The
   242	generic grid has unbounded numerator and \(M\); there is no completeness
   243	certificate for any \(b\ge 2\) entry.  
   244	**Side:** proof. Completeness by a well-quasi-order, not by a compact
   245	chart and not by a grammar.
   246	
   247	### Five-line pitch
   248	
   249	1. An off-axis fat record is a finite word in a finite event alphabet
   250	   (the priced menu P0 / St 9.6 families / merge schemas) together with
   250	   (the priced menu P0 / St 9.6 families / merge schemas) together with
   251	   a tuple of nonnegative integer registers \((M_i,\operatorname{num} w_i,\nu_i,\ldots)\).
   252	2. Higman's lemma well-quasi-orders the words; Dickson's lemma
   253	   well-quasi-orders the register tuples; the product well-quasi-orders
   254	   fat records under *minor* = “subword + coordinatewise \(\le\) on
   255	   registers.”
   256	3. If *death* is minor-closed (a dead minor of a live record is
   257	   forbidden — equivalently, liveness is an upset), then the live
   258	   records are a WQO-downset and there are only finitely many
   259	   *minimal* live records at each T7 entry. That finite set *is*
   260	   \(B_{\mathrm{off}}(d,E)\).
   261	4. The td-7 inversion of the family \(w=2/(2k+1)\) is the planted
   262	   positive example: those records form a Dickson *chain* in the
   263	   register \(k\), not an antichain, and the inversion is the
   264	   statement that the chain dies at a bounded minor.
   265	5. Unbounded numerator is then harmless. An infinite grid can still
   266	   have a finite live upset. P5's “no completeness certificate”
   267	   becomes “prove minor-closed, then enumerate the finite antichain of
   268	   minimal live records.”
   269	
   270	### Nearest neighbor, and why this is not it
   271	
   272	`xmodel/sol-accel2.md` contracts *already-killed* routes by forbidden
   273	minors; that is a dual of existing kills, not a completeness theorem
   274	for the live language. Sol's Rank 2 is a finitely presented grammar
   275	with symbolic `OPEN`s; it proves landing of a derivation, not finiteness
   276	of live records. Yesterday's #4 compactifies the characteristic; #5
   277	covers off-axis by a Kummer extension of the on-axis book. NF-Z/P/M
   278	quotients words. Contextual live-kernel (Sol Rank 5) quotients by
   279	future-equivalence in a *fixed* context. None of these is “liveness is
   280	an upset in a WQO.”
   281	
   282	### First concrete test
   283	
   284	1. Encode the td-7 §11a 17 cells and the 62 budget-fitting merge cells
   285	   of `BOOK-OFFAXIS.md` §10 as fat records: event word plus the
   286	   registers the priced menu actually consumes.
   287	2. On this finite sample, test the hereditary claim in both directions.
   288	   (a) If \(A\) is DEAD and \(B\) has \(A\) as a minor, is \(B\) DEAD?
   289	   (b) If \(B\) is LIVE and \(A\) is a minor of \(B\), is \(A\) LIVE?
   290	   One failure of (a) with a clear extra priced event in \(B\) is a
   291	   *repair* (minors must retain priced letters); one failure of (a) with
   292	   \(B\) obtained from \(A\) only by raising a register is a genuine
   293	   kill of death-as-minor-closed.
   294	3. Mandatory chain: the inverted family \(w=2/(2k+1)\). Confirm it is
   295	   a Dickson chain in \(k\), and that the filed closure is a minor of
   296	   every larger-\(k\) record. If those records are an *antichain*, the
   297	   embedding is wrong; change the register coordinates (the inversion
   298	   itself says \(T=1/k\) is the right one) and rerun.
   299	4. One td-11 A/B/C pure-neutral probe: the co-scaled \(5^k\) tuples
   300	   that die at a common earlier \(X\) (`xmodel/sol-lateral3.md` Rank 5's
   301	   planted example) must be a Dickson chain of dead records, not an
   302	   antichain of live ones.
   303	5. No Groebner. Output: a yes/no hereditary table, plus the embedding
   304	   that makes the td-7 inversion a chain.
   305	
   306	### Kill criterion
   307	
   308	Kill if the finite td-7 sample already contains a LIVE record with a
   309	DEAD register-only minor — then death is not minor-closed in the
   310	registers the engine actually uses, and WQO cannot enumerate the live
   311	set. Kill if there is an infinite antichain of *jointly live* records
   312	in one filed T7 entry (incomparable event words, all live at every
   313	register). A new event letter required by a printed St 9.6 family is a
   314	repair of the alphabet, not a kill. Do not claim completeness from
   315	Higman alone without the hereditary check: WQO of the ambient is free,
   316	and worthless.
