   159	
   160	## 2. The exact natural interface with pole mass
   161	
   162	At a pole \(P\), the promoted entry equations give
   163	
   164	\[
   165	 (D,D_g)=a(\alpha,\beta),\qquad
   166	 (P,P_g)=b(\alpha,\beta),
   167	\]
   168	
   169	\[
   170	 M=b,\qquad \bar\kappa=as,\qquad \rho=a/b,\qquad
   171	 \Lambda=\frac{\alpha\beta ab}{\nu}.
   172	\]
   173	
   174	See [`SHEET6-TDUNIFORM.md:36-74`](../SHEET6-TDUNIFORM.md) and
   175	[`SHEET6-MULTIPOLE.md:48-61`](../SHEET6-MULTIPOLE.md). Therefore
   176	
   177	\[
   178	 w=\frac{as-a/b}{\nu}
   179	   =\frac{a(sb-1)}{b\nu}.
   180	\]
   181	
   182	Solving for the normalized pole mass gives the exact formula
   183	
   184	\[
   185	 \boxed{
   186	   \frac{ab}{\nu}=\Phi_s(w,M)
   187	   :=\frac{wM^2}{sM-1}.}                              \tag{2.1}
   188	\]
   189	
   190	Proposition 5.8 then gives
   191	
   192	\[
   193	 \boxed{
   194	   \frac{td}{\alpha\beta}
   195	    =\sum_{P\ \mathrm{pole}}\frac{a_Pb_P}{\nu_P}
   196	    =\sum_{P\ \mathrm{pole}}\Phi_s(w_P,M_P).}         \tag{2.2}
   197	\]
   198	
   199	This is an exact memoryless scalar bridge from pole data to the FC5 state.
   200	It is exact **at poles**. At an internal chart,
   201	\(\Phi_s(w_G,M_G)\) is only a candidate potential; it is not a geometric
   202	mass supplied by any cited theorem.
   203	
   204	Any direct FC5 proof of TD-BOUND would therefore need, at minimum, a global
   205	telescope controlling the sum of the pole values in (2.2). The next two
   206	sections show exactly why the obvious telescope fails.
   207	
   208	---
   209	
   210	## 3. What FC5 does at an all-`mu=1` merge
   211	
   212	Suppose an all-\(\mu=1\), nonzero-edge merge receives the common depth-state
   213	value \(w\). The depth handshake gives
   214	
   215	\[
   216	 \bar\kappa_G=w\frac{d_q}{\Delta},\qquad
   217	 X_G=w\frac{d_p}{\Delta},\qquad
   218	 \Delta:=d_q-d_p.
   219	\]
   220	
   221	Combining this with FC5 yields
   222	
   223	\[
   224	 \boxed{
   225	 w_{\rm out}=w\frac{d_q-1}{\nu\Delta}.}                \tag{3.1}
   226	\]
   227	
   228	For the three MP6 families this specializes to
   229	
   230	\[
   230	\[
   231	\begin{array}{c|c|c}
   232	\text{family}&(d_p,d_q)&w_{\rm out}/w\\ \hline
   233	\mathrm{IIa}&(r\nu,(r+l)\nu+1)&(r+l)/(l\nu+1)\\
   234	\mathrm{ZCH}&((r-1)\nu+1,(r-1+l)\nu+1)&(r-1+l)/(l\nu)\\
   235	\mathrm{I}&(r,r+l)&(r+l-1)/l.
   236	\end{array}                                             \tag{3.2}
   237	\]
   238	
   239	Every factor is at most \(r\), but the factors occur on both sides of one.
   240	Along a fixed hierarchy this supplies only a finite growth estimate, roughly
   241	at most a product of arrival counts. The number of leaves, the initial
   242	\(w\), and the hierarchy itself are already `td`-dependent. Equation (3.2)
   243	therefore has no reverse leverage on the entry mass.
   244	
   245	### Proposition 2 (a real but wrong-way IIa inequality)
   246	
   247	**PROVED.** For an all-\(\mu=1\) IIa merge, put
   248	
   249	\[
   250	 \Delta=l\nu+1,\qquad g=\gcd(r,\Delta)=M_{\rm out}.
   251	\]
   252	
   253	Compare the potential of \(r\) equal incoming `M=1` states with the
   254	potential of the emitted state:
   255	
   256	\[
   257	 \frac{\delta}{w}
   258	 :=\frac{r}{s-1}
   259	   -\frac{(r+l)g^2}{\Delta(sg-1)}.                     \tag{3.3}
   260	\]
   261	
   262	If the cell is nonsaturated, \(\Delta\nmid r\), then \(\delta>0\).
   263	
   264	Indeed write \(r=gR\), \(\Delta=gD\). Nonsaturation gives \(D\ge2\),
   265	and \(\nu\ge2\) gives \(l\le(gD-1)/2\). After clearing positive
   266	denominators, (3.3) is positive exactly when
   267	
   268	\[
   269	 RD(sg-1)>(gR+l)(s-1).                                 \tag{3.4}
   270	\]
   271	
   272	The coefficient of \(R\) in the left-minus-right side is
   273	
   274	\[
   275	 A_R=D(sg-1)-g(s-1).
   276	\]
   277	
   278	For \(g=1\), \(A_R=(s-1)(D-1)>0\). For \(g\ge2,D\ge2\), it is
   279	minimized at \(D=2\), where \(A_R=g(s+1)-2>0\). The difference is
   280	therefore increasing in \(R\), so it suffices to take \(R=1\). Twice
   281	the resulting difference is bounded below by
   282	
   283	\[
   284	 gs(D-2)+D(g-2)+2g+s-1>0,                              \tag{3.5}
   285	\]
   286	
   287	with the displayed lower bound in the `g=1` case reducing to
   288	\((s-1)(D-1)>0\).
   289	
   290	Thus every nonsaturated IIa cell dissipates the natural potential.
   291	
   292	The general saturated-IIa coefficient kill is currently recorded only at
   293	the quarantined A3 tier in
   294	[`SHEET6-MULTIPOLE.md:145-151`](../SHEET6-MULTIPOLE.md).
   295	Treating that general kill as promoted is therefore **CONJECTURE** in this
   296	note. Even if it is granted, Proposition 2 gives
   297	
   298	\[
   299	 \sum \Phi_s(\text{incoming})
   300	   \;\ge\;\Phi_s(\text{outgoing}),                     \tag{3.6}
   301	\]
   302	
   303	which is the wrong direction. A terminal lower bound would yield another
   304	lower bound on leaf mass. TD-BOUND needs an upper bound on the accumulated
   305	dissipation plus the terminal contribution, and no such budget occurs in
   306	FC5.
   307	
