   310	## 4. The natural potential is nonmonotone even after local coefficient solving
   311	
   312	Two exact controls dispose of a simple monotonicity proof.
   313	
   314	### 4.1 Decrease: residue A
   315	
   316	For \(s=5\), the two residue-A pole states are \((w,M)=(2,1)\), so their
   317	total pole mass is
   318	
   319	\[
   320	 2\Phi_5(2,1)=1.
   321	\]
   322	
   323	The coefficient-solvable local IIa chart
   324	\((r,\nu,l)=(2,3,1)\) emits
   325	
   326	\[
   327	 (w',M')=(3/2,2),\qquad \Phi_5(3/2,2)=2/3.             \tag{4.1}
   328	\]
   329	
   330	The potential decreases by \(1/3\).
   331	
   332	### 4.2 Increase: the NF-M BB2 cylinder
   333	
   334	The type-\((2,3)\) `11-C` packet has two B inputs
   335	\((w,M)=(3/2,2)\); see
   336	[`TOWER-TD11.md:104-115`](../TOWER-TD11.md). Their total is
   337	
   338	\[
   339	 2\Phi_5(3/2,2)=4/3.
   340	\]
   341	
   342	For every \(N\ge2\), NF-M's coefficient-solvable BB2 cylinder has
   343	
   344	\[
   345	 (\bar\kappa,d_p,d_q)=(6N+3,4N+1,2N+1),\qquad M'=1.
   346	\]
   347	
   348	FC5 gives
   349	
   350	\[
   351	 w'=\frac{(6N+3)(2N)}{N(2N+1)}=6,\qquad
   352	 \Phi_5(6,1)=3/2.                                      \tag{4.2}
   353	\]
   354	
   355	The potential increases by \(1/6\). This arithmetic schema has a solved
   356	local coefficient fiber: NF-M finds the unique type `b=-a` for every \(N\); see
   357	[`NF-M.md:170-188`](../NF-M.md). The chart later dies by a separate
   358	denominator criterion. That later death does not turn FC5 into a monotone
   359	law.
   360	
   361	Equations (4.1)--(4.2) prove:
   362	
   363	> **PROVED NO-TELESCOPE STATEMENT.** The pole-mass extension
   364	> \(\Phi_s(w,M)\) obeys neither one-step inequality
   365	> \(\Phi_{\rm out}\le\sum\Phi_{\rm in}\) nor its reverse uniformly
   366	> across coefficient-solvable local merge charts.
   367	
   368	All three state values used here satisfy the type-`(2,3)` pole laws:
   369	`(2,1)` comes from `(a,b,nu)=(1,1,2)`, `(3/2,2)` from
   370	`(1,2,3)`, and `(6,1)` from `(3,1,2)`. Hence the same controls rule
   371	out any universal memoryless state potential `V(w,M)` that must equal pole
   372	mass on every pole-law state and obey one fixed local inequality on every
   373	coefficient-solvable chart in this relaxation.
   374	
   375	This does not rule out a signed cancellation over globally admissible
   376	histories, a global restriction eliminating one sign, or a richer internal
   377	state. It rules out the direct one-step monotonicity interpretation of FC5's
   378	emitted pair.
   379	
