   382	## 5. An unbounded fixed-type formal arithmetic-schema family
   383	
   384	This is the decisive obstruction to *any* numerical `td` bound from the
   385	listed local consequences alone.
   386	
   387	### Proposition 3 (unbounded local arithmetic schemas)
   388	
   389	**PROVED AS AN ARITHMETIC-SCHEMA STATEMENT; COMPATIBLE TREE/CHART AND GLOBAL
   390	REALIZATION ARE CONJECTURE/UNKNOWN.** Let
   391	
   392	\[
   393	 A=30t+5\quad(t\ge0),\qquad N:=9A-2,\qquad n:=13A-3.
   394	\]
   395	
   396	There is a one-pole type-\((2,3)\) record and one case-IIa numerical schema
   397	satisfying all identities listed below, with
   398	
   399	\[
   400	 td=4A\longrightarrow\infty,\qquad
   401	 \frac{ab}{\nu_0}=\frac{2A}{3}\longrightarrow\infty,  \tag{5.1}
   402	\]
   403	
   404	but with constant FC5 output
   405	
   406	\[
   407	 (w_1,M_1)=(2/3,3).                                    \tag{5.2}
   408	\]
   409	
   410	#### 5.1 Pole and entry laws
   411	
   412	Take
   413	
   414	\[
   415	 (\alpha,\beta)=(2,3),\qquad (a,b,\nu_0)=(A,2,3).
   416	\]
   417	
   418	The pole equations give
   419	
   420	\[
   421	 (D_0,D_{g,0})=(2A,3A),\qquad (P_0,P_{g,0})=(4,6),
   422	\]
   423	
   424	\[
   425	 \Lambda_0=td=\frac{6\cdot A\cdot2}{3}=4A,\qquad
   426	 M_0=b=2.                                               \tag{5.3}
   427	\]
   428	
   429	The Sigray entry menu is also satisfied:
   430	
   431	\[
   432	 \nu_0=3\mid\beta,\qquad
   433	 \nu_0\mid(b\alpha-1)=3.                             \tag{5.3a}
   434	\]
   435	
   436	The entry frame is
   437	
   438	\[
   439	 Q_0=(2A,4,3,2,5A),\qquad
   440	 \rho_0=A/2,\qquad w_0=3A/2.                          \tag{5.4}
   441	\]
   442	
   443	N1 holds because \(A\equiv2\pmod3\), so
   444	\(\gcd(\bar\kappa_0,\nu_0)=\gcd(5A,3)=1\).
   445	
   446	#### 5.2 Exact displayed Proposition 9.3 arithmetic
   447	
   448	Take a case-IIa step with
   449	
   450	\[
   451	 \mu=2,\qquad k=1,\qquad l=0,
   452	\]
   453	
   454	where \(\mu=2\mid M_0=2\); this is the required `k>0 => l=0` IIa
   455	pattern. With child pattern parameter \(\nu_1=N\), the reduced child degrees
   456	are
   457	
   458	\[
   459	 d_p=(k+\mu)N=3N=27A-6,\qquad
   460	 d_q=(k+l+1)N+1=2N+1=18A-3.                           \tag{5.5}
   461	\]
   462	
   463	Since \(i=P_0/\mu=2\), the exact case-I/II ratio is
   464	
   465	\[
   466	 \frac{d_p}{d_q}
   467	 =\frac{2(\rho_0+n)}{\bar\kappa_0+n}
   468	 =\frac{A+2(13A-3)}{5A+13A-3}
   469	 =\frac{27A-6}{18A-3}.                                \tag{5.6}
   470	\]
   471	
   472	Also \(\bar\kappa_0+n=18A-3\) is divisible by \(\nu_0=3\). The
   473	transport equations therefore give
   474	
   475	\[
   476	 D_1=\frac{D_0+nP_0}{3}=2N,\qquad P_1=i d_p=6N,
   477	\]
   478	
   479	\[
   480	 \rho_1=1/3,\qquad \nu_1=N,\qquad
   481	 \bar\kappa_1=\frac{\bar\kappa_0+n}{3}=6A-1.          \tag{5.7}
   482	\]
   483	
   484	Moreover
   485	
   486	\[
   487	 M_1=\gcd(3N,2N+1)=3,\qquad
   488	 \gcd(N,6A-1)=1,                                      \tag{5.8}
   489	\]
   490	
   491	the second identity following from
   492	\(2N-3(6A-1)=-1\). Thus child N1 also holds.
   493	
   494	The case-I/II equations used here are the H1-tier Proposition 9.3
   495	arithmetic (Sigray, pp. 50--51), summarized in
   496	[`SHEET6-CAMPAIGN.md:27-46`](../SHEET6-CAMPAIGN.md). The campaign explicitly
   497	records that trust perimeter at `SHEET6-CAMPAIGN.md:69-75`; no stronger
   498	incidence claim is imported here.
   499	
   500	#### 5.3 The arithmetic relaxation lets FC5 erase the scale
   501	
   502	Since \(d_q=3(6A-1)\),
   503	
   504	\[
   505	 \rho_1=\frac{\bar\kappa_1}{d_q}=\frac13,
   506	\]
   507	
   508	and FC5 gives
   509	
   510	\[
   511	 w_1
   512	 =\frac{\bar\kappa_1(d_q-1)}{N d_q}
   513	 =\frac{6A-1}{18A-3}\frac{2N}{N}
   514	 =\frac23.                                             \tag{5.9}
   515	\]
   516	
   517	Thus the displayed arithmetic-schema relaxation maps (5.1) to the constant
   518	state (5.2). No inverse estimate from `(w_1,M_1)` to the pole mass can follow
   519	within that relaxation. A realized Keller chart could still carry additional
   520	global constraints not represented by this state.
   521	
   522	#### 5.4 Necessary characteristic-denominator arithmetic
   523	
   524	To check more than the ratio equation, choose
   525	
   526	\[
   527	 \kappa_0=3N,\qquad
   528	 \pi_0=\frac{22A-6}{3N},
   529	\]
   530	
   531	\[
   532	 \kappa_1=N,\qquad
   533	 \pi_1=\frac{3A-1}{N}.
   534	\]
   535	
   536	Then \(\bar\kappa_j=\kappa_j(1-\pi_j)\), and
   537	
   538	\[
   539	 \pi_0-\pi_1=\frac{n}{3N}.                             \tag{5.10}
   540	\]
   541	
   542	For \(A=30t+5\),
   543	
   544	\[
   545	 \gcd(3N,22A-6)=1,\qquad
   546	 \gcd(3N,9A-3)=3.                                     \tag{5.11}
   547	\]
   548	
   549	Hence the two reduced denominators are exactly \(3N\) and \(N\), the
   550	expected possible factor-three characteristic gcd chain. This is necessary
   551	Puiseux-lattice arithmetic; it proves neither that the two coefficient
   552	patterns occur on one compatible local chart transition nor that these are
   553	consecutive vertices in a globally realized tree.
   554	
   555	#### 5.5 Charge and terminal numerical gates
   556	
   557	The `k=1` extra orbit has positive gap
   558	
   559	\[
   560	 \frac{D_1}{i}-\bar\kappa_1
   900	
   901	Hence every row with \(q>\alpha\) violates TD-BOUND before any tower is
   902	built. If \(q=\alpha\), equality would require every pole to be
