   921	
   922	## 10. The exact global coordinate: mismatch energy
   923	
   924	The existing resolved-pencil calculation identifies precisely where new
   925	mathematics must enter.
   926	
   927	Let
   928	
   929	\[
   930	 \deg f=B\alpha,\qquad \deg g=B\beta.
   931	\]
   932	
   933	Resolve the two projective pencils simultaneously. For each proper or
   934	infinitely-near base center \(p\), let \(R_p,S_p\) be the multiplicities of
   935	generic members of the two pencils, using zero when a center belongs only to
   936	one. In the orthogonal total-transform basis,
   937	
   938	\[
   939	 C=B\alpha H-\sum_pR_pE_p^*,\qquad
   940	 D=B\beta H-\sum_pS_pE_p^*.
   941	\]
   942	
   943	Because general pencil fibers have square zero and meet in the affine plane
   944	in `td` points,
   945	
   946	\[
   947	 \sum_pR_p^2=B^2\alpha^2,\qquad
   948	 \sum_pS_p^2=B^2\beta^2,\qquad
   949	 \sum_pR_pS_p=B^2\alpha\beta-td.
   950	\]
   951	
   952	Expanding the square gives the **PROVED** identity
   953	
   954	\[
   955	 \boxed{
   956	 \frac{td}{\alpha\beta}
   957	 =\frac12\sum_p
   958	 \left(\frac{R_p}{\alpha}-\frac{S_p}{\beta}\right)^2.} \tag{10.1}
   959	\]
   960	
   961	Combining with the pole formula gives
   962	
   963	\[
   964	 \boxed{
   965	 \sum_P\frac{a_Pb_P}{\nu_P}
   966	 =\frac12\sum_p
   967	 \left(\frac{R_p}{\alpha}-\frac{S_p}{\beta}\right)^2.} \tag{10.2}
   968	\]
   969	
   970	Equivalently, for
   971	
   972	\[
   973	 \Delta:=C/\alpha-D/\beta,
   974	\]
   975	
   976	\[
   977	 \Delta^2=-\frac{2td}{\alpha\beta}.                   \tag{10.3}
   978	\]
   979	
   980	The derivation is recorded in
   981	[`xmodel/sol-tdbound-review.md:349-427`](sol-tdbound-review.md). No local
   982	identification of an individual pole summand with an individual resolution
   983	center is currently proved.
   984	
   985	### Exact equivalent global reformulation
   986	
   987	> **CONJECTURE KME-2 (Keller mismatch-energy bound).** For every
   988	> Sigray-normalized polynomial Keller pair of type \((\alpha,\beta)\), on a
   989	> common resolution of its two projective pencils,
   990	> \[
   991	>   \sum_p\left(\frac{R_p}{\alpha}
   992	>                    -\frac{S_p}{\beta}\right)^2\le2. \tag{KME-2}
   993	> \]
   994	
   995	By (10.1)--(10.3), KME-2 is exactly equivalent to
   996	
   997	\[
   998	 \sum_P\frac{a_Pb_P}{\nu_P}\le1,\qquad
   999	 td\le\alpha\beta,\qquad \Delta^2\ge-2.              \tag{10.4}
  1000	\]
  1001	
  1002	KME-2 is not an independent mechanism lemma: by (10.1), it is TD-BOUND
  1003	rewritten in exact global coordinates. Its value is diagnostic. It proves
  1004	that the missing mechanism must control normalized divergence energy, but it
  1005	does not by itself advance the inequality.
  1006	
  1007	**Hardness: 9/10.** The exceptional lattice alone does not imply KME-2.
  1008	Even support on an ADE configuration is insufficient: multiples of a root
  1009	have arbitrarily negative square. The theorem must constrain the
  1010	coefficients of the normalized difference divisor, using the constant
  1011	Jacobian and proximity relations at the centers where normalized
  1012	multiplicities diverge.
  1013	
  1014	### A more structural sufficient target
  1015	
  1016	The following statement is stronger than KME-2 but gives a concrete bridge
  1017	from common-power contacts to the required global inequality.
  1018	
  1019	> **CONJECTURE PCC (proportional-center coverage).** At every resolved base
  1020	> center put
  1021	> \[
  1022	> h_p:=\min\!\left(
  1023	>       \left\lfloor\frac{R_p}{\alpha}\right\rfloor,
  1024	>       \left\lfloor\frac{S_p}{\beta}\right\rfloor
  1025	>       \right).
  1026	> \]
  1027	> Then
  1028	> \[
  1029	> \sum_ph_p^2\ge B^2-1.                               \tag{PCC}
  1030	> \]
  1031	
  1032	If PCC holds, then
  1033	
  1034	\[
  1035	 I_\infty=\sum_pR_pS_p
  1036	 \ge\alpha\beta\sum_ph_p^2
  1037	 \ge\alpha\beta(B^2-1),
  1038	\]
  1039	
  1040	and hence `td <= alpha*beta`. Corollary 7.4's common powers are plausible
  1041	sources of the \(h_p\), but the needed weighted/Laurent-to-projective
  1042	transport, proximity accounting, distinct-center coverage, and no-double-
  1043	counting theorem do not exist in the repository.
  1044	
  1045	**Hardness: 8.5/10.** This is the most concrete proof lane found. FC5 could
  1046	participate only after an emission-to-proximity theorem says which base
  1047	centers and which source increments a chart represents. Its current pair
  1048	`(w,M)` does not contain that information.
  1049	
  1050	### The smallest sector lemma an emission proof must import
  1051	
  1052	> **CONJECTURE SP (single-pole cap).** If a one-pole Sigray configuration is
  1053	> globally realizable by a polynomial Keller pair, then
  1054	> \[
  1055	>   ab\le\nu.                                           \tag{SP}
  1056	> \]
  1057	
  1058	SP is exactly TD-BOUND in the one-pole sector. It is necessary for any
  1059	merge-based proof, because there is no multi-leaf merge in that sector. The
  1060	family in Proposition 3 satisfies the current local rules while violating SP
  1061	by an unbounded factor.
  1062	
  1063	KME-2 already implies SP as its one-pole specialization; SP is not an
  1064	additional hypothesis if KME-2 is assumed. It is isolated because it is the
  1065	smallest necessary sector test for any proposed emission-based mechanism.
  1066	
  1067	**Hardness: 8/10.** A proof must use global gluing/algebraization or force
  1068	unbooked charge. Refining FC5 cannot prove SP because FC5 has no incoming
  1069	merge to compare.
  1070	
  1071	### Relaxed finite typewise target
  1072	
  1073	Even a nonsharp finite bound would require:
  1074	
  1075	> **CONJECTURE KME-finite.** For each fixed \((\alpha,\beta)\), there is an
  1076	> effective constant \(C_{\alpha,\beta}<\infty\), independent of the degree
  1077	> scale and tree, such that
  1078	> \[
  1079	>   \sum_p\left(\frac{R_p}{\alpha}
  1080	>                    -\frac{S_p}{\beta}\right)^2
  1160	> a priori explicitly computable integer \(B_0\), fixed independently of
  1161	> any unknown counterexample and independently of its unknown GGV minimum,
  1162	> such that: if a plane Keller counterexample exists, some globally minimal
  1163	> GGV pair selected for the reduction has transported Sigray type satisfying
  1164	> \[
  1165	>   \alpha\beta\le B_0                              \tag{SC}
  1166	> \]
  1167	
  1168	KME-2 plus SC would give `td <= B_0` for that selected pair and would avoid
  1169	the iteration obstruction, because it does not claim a bound for every
  1170	iterate. A direct selected-pair `td` bound would serve the same purpose.
  1171	
  1172	**Hardness: 10/10.** No current GGV or sheet theorem supplies an upper type
  1173	or degree bound. In addition, cofinal enumeration still needs a
  1174	completeness-preserving fixed-rung symbolic quotient: NF-Z, NF-P, generalized
  1175	NF-M, off-axis `b>=2` termination, and all full-route landing sectors.
