   216	  \[
   217	    x\le \left\lfloor{S+l-1\over\epsilon}\right\rfloor-1-k. \tag{2.3}
   218	  \]
   219	
   220	* If `epsilon=0`, then `T=S+l` and, since `nu>=2`,
   221	
   222	  \[
   223	    2\{l(k+x)-S\}+l\le E\le la(S+l),                     \tag{2.4}
   224	  \]
   225	
   226	  which gives an explicit finite upper bound for `x`.
   227	* Having fixed these data, (2.2) gives finitely many `E`, and
   228	  `nu=(E-l+epsilon)/C` is determined.
   229	
   230	This proves the missing budget-dependent `k`, multiplicity-partition, and
   231	`ell_ex` stopping rule for every dirty **one-step** P0 expansion.  The hard
   232	loops `k<7` and `ell_ex<41` in the exploratory td-7 code have no place in the
   233	new compiler.
   234	
   235	Changing clean resonances are also finite for each fixed concrete `w`: with
   236	`Delta=(n-1)nu+1`, integrality gives `Delta | num(w)` and a changing step has
   237	`w' = w*n/Delta <= 2w/3`.  Thus the numerator strictly descends.  Strict
   238	neutral `M`-drops traverse only the finite divisor poset of `M`.  Every
   239	`M`-preserving neutral step still changes its endpoint frame and scale and
   240	remains in cylinder (2.5); it is never erased as stuttering.  Since every
   241	non-clean step has price at least one, there are at most `td-2` such steps on
   242	a gross-budget route (at most 9 for td-11 and 11 for td-13).
   243	
   244	Once a complete ledger constraint system is supplied, budget coordinates add
   245	no fourth unbounded family: every positive integer `lambda` lies in
   246	`[1,b]`, there are at most `b` positive atoms, and their slack/inventory
   247	assignments are retained as a finite feasible relation.  If the available
   248	theorems provide only lower bounds and do not determine that relation, the
   249	record is `UNRESOLVED_BUDGET_INVENTORY` and cannot support an emptiness
   250	certificate; this is missing input authority, not permission to use the
   251	minimum-price projection.
   252	
   253	Only two one-step chain cylinders remain unbounded.
   254	
   255	**Neutral cylinder (`n=1`).**
   256	
   257	\[
   258	 w'=w,\quad M'=\gcd(l,u+1),\quad
   259	 (\nu',\bar\kappa',\rho',P')=(u,w(u+1),w,Pu).             \tag{2.5}
   260	\]
   261	
   262	Here and in (2.6), `u>=2`.  If `w=a/d` is reduced, vertex integrality and
   263	BOOK-N1 are exactly
   264	`d | u+1` and `gcd(a,u)=1`.  Together with the prescribed `M'`, these are a
   265	finite union of residue classes modulo `lcm(l,a,d)`.
   266	
   267	**Pure-(b) cylinder (`1 <= epsilon < l`, no extras).**  With
   268	`e=l-epsilon`,
   269	
   270	\[
   271	 w'={lw\over e},\quad M'=\gcd(e,u+1),\quad
   272	 (\nu',\bar\kappa',\rho')=(u,w'(u+1),w'),
   273	\]
   274	
   275	\[
   276	 P'=P{\epsilon+lu\over l},\qquad
   277	 \lambda\ge\left\lceil{lw\over\epsilon}\right\rceil.  \tag{2.6}
   278	\]
   279	
   280	Writing `w'=a'/d'` reduced, the allowed `u` again form finitely many residue
   281	classes: `d' | u+1`, `gcd(a',u)=1`, and
   282	`gcd(e,u+1)=M'`.  The parameter is nevertheless unbounded, and unlike a
   283	neutral loop it occurs at one positive-price event.
   284	
   285	Consequently P0 has a finite non-parametric skeleton plus the exact cylinders
   286	(2.5)-(2.6).  This is stronger than the old capped closure, but it is not yet
   287	a finite future quotient of their scale parameters.
   288	
   289	### 2.2 Discrete merge reduction
   290	
   291	The same calculation gives a useful finite reduction before the coefficient
   292	ODE is solved.  Fix incoming exact records and a zero-slot choice.  This
   293	subsection first treats `nu>=2`.  The exceptional `nu=1` branch is Prop. 9.3
   294	case I even when a zero-chain arrives; its eta-absorbed convention and its
   295	legal eta-factor subvariant are different schemas.  No finite reduction of
   296	those schemas is claimed here: they are retained verbatim and assigned to
   297	CONJECTURE NF-P below.  For `nu>=2`, let
   298	
   299	\[
   300	 A=\sum_{\text{nonzero arrivals}}\mu_e+\sum_{j=1}^k m_j,
   301	 \qquad Q=r_0+k+x,
   302	\]
   303	
   304	so that
   305	
   306	\[
   307	 d_p=\epsilon+\nu A,\qquad d_q=1+\nu Q.                  \tag{2.7}
   308	\]
   309	
   310	Here `r_0` counts nonzero arriving orbits and `x=ell_ex`.  There are finitely
   311	many arrival multiplicities because `mu_e | M_e`; `r_0 <= m`; budget gives
   312	`k <= b`; and the NE inequalities give
   313	`m_j < min_e(mu_e)` (and the analogous finite bound on a free zero-root
   314	multiplicity).
   315	
   316	Each edge supplies an affine handshake in `(kbar_G,X_G)`:
   317	
   318	\[
   319	 X_G=\mu_e(\bar\kappa_G-w_e),\qquad
   320	 X_G=\mu_0(\bar\kappa_G-\nu_e w_e)\ \text{on a zero edge}. \tag{2.8}
   321	\]
   322	
   323	If two affine rows differ, they either contradict or pin `(kbar_G,X_G)`.
   324	Writing the pinned ratio `X_G/kbar_G=p/q` in lowest terms, (2.7) gives
   325	
   326	\[
   327	 \nu(qA-pQ)=p-q\epsilon.                                 \tag{2.9}
   328	\]
   329	
   330	The right side cannot vanish: that would say `d_p=epsilon*d_q`, forbidden by
   331	R1.0's root-multiplicity law.  Hence `qA-pQ` is a signed divisor of the fixed
   332	nonzero right side; `x` and `nu` are determined from finitely many divisors.
   333	
   334	The only unpinned case is when every affine row is the same: all arrivals
   335	have one multiplicity `mu` and one effective weight `h` (where a zero edge
   336	uses `h=nu_e*w_e`).  Put `h=a/b` reduced and
   337	
   338	\[
   339	 C=\mu Q-A,\qquad E=\mu d_q-d_p=\mu-\epsilon+\nu C,
   340	 \qquad
   341	 \bar\kappa_G={a\mu(1+\nu Q)\over bE}.                   \tag{2.10}
   342	\]
   343	
   344	For `nu>=2`, integrality bounds every non-pure case:
   345	
   346	* if `epsilon=0`, the identity
   347	  `C*dq-Q*E=-(A-epsilon*Q)` gives `E | a*mu*A`;
   348	* if `0<epsilon<mu` is a free NE zero root,
   349	  `A-epsilon*Q>0` bounds `Q`, and then the same identity bounds `nu` unless
   350	  `C=0`;
   351	* if `epsilon=mu` is an arriving zero edge, `E=nu*C`; integrality in
   352	  (2.10) gives `nu | a*mu` and `C | a*(nu*A+mu)`.
   353	
   354	For auditability, these divisibilities do not assume a hidden characteristic
   355	cap.  Integrality of (2.10) says `b*E | a*mu*dq`; multiplying
   356	`C*dq-Q*E=-(A-epsilon*Q)` by `a*mu` gives the first two bounds.  In the last
   357	case, reduction modulo `nu` gives `nu | a*mu`, while
   358	`mu*dq=nu*A+mu+nu*C` gives the displayed divisor bound for `C`.  Moreover
   359	
   360	\[
   361	 C=\mu(k+x)-\sum_j m_j
   362	   =\mu x+\sum_j(\mu-m_j),                              \tag{2.11}
   363	\]
   364	
   365	so `C=0` is exactly `k=x=0` under the strict NE inequalities.
   366	
   367	Thus only one parametric shape survives the proved `nu>=2` arithmetic
   368	reduction, while one exceptional family is deliberately left unreduced:
   369	
   370	1. `C=0`, necessarily with no nonzero NE or q-extra orbit, giving a pure
   371	   `nu`-cylinder (with or without one free zero root);
   372	2. **UNREDUCED:** `nu=1` case-I schemas, retaining both the eta-absorbed and
   373	   legal eta-factor variants, where rational `kbar` can allow an `x`-tail
   374	   such as td-7's `(2,2t)` family.
   375	
   376	All pinned `nu>=2` joins (including pinned examples with every `mu>=2`)
   377	therefore have a finite discrete menu for fixed incoming records.
   378	Equal-handshake `nu>=2` joins have a finite menu plus the pure cylinder.
   379	Retaining every case-I/eta branch separately is conservative and cap-free,
   380	but its finite canonicalization is unproved.  Thus this subsection proves
   381	finite discrete **schemas only for `nu>=2`**; it does **not** prove a finite
   382	coefficient type or a terminating projection when the incoming records
   383	themselves contain symbolic scale parameters.
   384	
   385	### 2.3 What the depth theorem contributes
   386	
   387	On an M=1 merge-free segment, the promoted depth theorem proves a finite
   388	`w`-alphabet and finite **cumulative jump-cell menu**.  It does not prove that
   389	equal `w` (or equal `(w,M,price)`) gives equal E5F/tower futures.  Its safe
   390	proved representative bound is
   390	proved representative bound is
   391	
   392	\[
   393	 d_0\le 2\,\mathrm{gen}(W)+2,                             \tag{2.12}
   394	\]
   395	
   396	not the sharper `gen(W)+2` still printed in the body of
   397	`SHEET6-DEPTH.md`; see `SHEET6-DEPTH-REVIEW.md:183-218,245-267`.
   398	Equation (2.12) may be used only for the menu-existence projection.  It may
   399	not truncate endpoint frames, degree products, E5 pads, or tower histories.
   400	
   401	## 3. The exact remaining lemma list
   402	
   403	These three obligations are independent relative to the cited kernels: NF-Z
   404	already occurs with no charged step, NF-P occurs in one fixed-depth charged
   405	step, and NF-M remains after every integer characteristic is fixed.
   406	
   407	### CONJECTURE NF-Z — neutral-word future quotient
   408	
   409	For every fixed `(td,entry,hierarchy)` and bounded non-neutral skeleton, the
   410	arbitrary word of neutral cells (2.5) admits one of finitely many effective
   411	canonical **symbolic summary schemas**, with exact parameter domains and a
   412	closed concatenation operation, such that a schema specialization determines
   413	exactly:
   414	
   415	1. the endpoint `(nu,kbar,rho,M)` and every legal cost-coupled E5F reroute;
   416	2. the total full-degree scale needed by equal quotient and H8;
   417	3. all tower gap, live-factor cap, and prefix-delta predicates contributed by
   418	   every neutral vertex, not merely their maximum under a td-7 window; and
   419	4. the result of attaching any remaining charged/merge skeleton.
   420	
   421	N1 and N4 are useful update identities, not a proof of this statement.  N4
   422	controls gaps monotonically; it does not by itself quotient the unbounded
   423	degree product or all H8/cap congruences.  A proof may use a finite transition
   424	monoid of consumer predicates, but it must exhibit that monoid and prove it
   425	closed.  A depth sample or `d0` menu representative is insufficient.
   426	
   427	### CONJECTURE NF-P — parameter/exceptional-schema quotient
   428	
   429	Every bounded collection of free parameters from pure-(b) cells (2.6), pure
   430	equal-handshake merge cylinders, and **every** `nu=1` case-I merge schema
   431	(including the eta-absorbed and legal eta-factor variants) admits an
   432	effective finite canonical partition on which P0 divisibility, current `M`,
   433	E5F, degree synchronization, and tower predicates have exact symbolic
   434	updates and decidable emptiness.  This includes proving a complete discrete
   435	schema and correct case-I handshakes for `nu=1`.
   436	
   437	The partition must also be closed under the **full reflexive-transitive
   438	closure of the non-neutral/state-changing P0 relation**, including every
   439	zero-cost changing clean resonance generated by a parameterized `w`, and must
   440	return only finitely many exact successor summaries.  Each intervening
   441	`M`-preserving neutral block is represented by the NF-Z summary.  Pointwise
   442	numerator descent for each concrete specialization does not imply this
   443	uniform symbolic closure.
   444	
   445	This is not NF-Z: a single positive-price pure-(b) vertex has an unbounded
   446	characteristic even at depth one, and it changes both the frame and full
   447	degree by the affine factor `(epsilon+l*nu)/l`.  The existing conjecture
   448	“pure-b characteristic invariance” at
   449	`xmodel/sol-gluing-design.md:1149-1154` is a special case, not a proof.
   450	
   451	### CONJECTURE NF-M — multi-orbit coefficient locality
   452	
   453	For every fixed discrete merge schema from §2.2 **or produced by NF-P**,
   454	including every `nu=1` eta mode and every parametric cylinder, the exact
   455	Prop. 8.1(iv) equation modulo orbit permutation and the allowed scaling has a
   456	finite computable certificate type with this property:
   457	two solutions of the same type and the same emitted frame have identical
   458	chain/E5F/tower futures.  The procedure must either return an impossibility
   459	certificate or retain every coefficient/root label that a later edge can
   460	observe.
   461	
   462	This is the missing generalized multi-factor ODE theorem.  Solving one fixed
   463	cell does not prove it uniformly in a pure `nu`- or `x`-tail, and forgetting a
   464	positive-dimensional solution family without a locality proof is not sound.
   465	
   466	### Conditional completeness theorem
   467	
   468	**Theorem (conditional).** If NF-Z, NF-P, and NF-M hold, then for each fixed
   469	`(m,td)` the records (1.2)-(1.3) have a finite, computable,
   470	completeness-preserving symbolic quotient.
   471	
   472	**Proof.**  The entry and labelled hierarchy sets are finite, with at most
   473	`m-1` merge nodes.  Section 2.1 bounds every non-parametric P0 choice and the
   474	number of positive-price atoms; for each concrete rational `w`, a changing
   475	clean resonance strictly decreases its reduced numerator.  Section 2.2
   476	reduces every `nu>=2` merge to finitely many fixed schemas and a pure
   477	cylinder.  NF-Z replaces every arbitrary neutral word by a member of a finite
   478	closed summary set; NF-P handles the bounded number of free integer
   479	cylinders, every exceptional `nu=1` schema, and their **uniform** clean-
   480	resonance closures; NF-M replaces every local coefficient family by a finite
   481	future-local type.  The exact transition formulas of §1 then map a finite set
   482	of canonical records to a finite set.  Induction over the hierarchy proves
   483	that every concrete route specializes one record and that every symbolic
   484	transition specializes only legal concrete transitions.  Exact Markov
   485	sufficiency (§1.2) proves that quotienting equal records loses no future.
   486	QED, conditional on the three labelled conjectures.
   487	
   488	These are three distinct obligation classes after the complete symbolic P0
   489	closure has been folded into NF-P and exact budget/inventory constraints have
   490	been made part of `L`.  None of the three conclusions follows from the cited
   491	kernels or from either of the other two as stated: without NF-Z there is
   492	unbounded zero-cost depth; without NF-P there is an unbounded characteristic
   493	at one charged vertex and an unclassified `nu=1` merge; without NF-M there is
   494	unclassified coefficient data even after all integer parameters are frozen.
   495	
   496	## 4. Worked example: td-11 entry 11-A
   497	
   498	The entry packet is
   499	
   500	\[
   501	 [M=1,w=2;P=2]\quad+\quad[M=2,w=3;P=4]
   502	\]
   503	
   504	of type `(2,3)` (`xmodel/sol-td11-13-scope.md:290-296`).
   505	
   506	### 4.1 Strict branch
   507	
   508	The first branch has `M=1,w=2`.  MP5 makes every later pre-merge vertex a
   509	simple clean cell.  Since `W(2)={2}`, no changing clean resonance exists.
   510	For a neutral characteristic `u`,
   511	
   512	\[
   513	 (\bar\kappa,\rho,w)=(2(u+1),2,2).
   514	\]
   515	
   516	BOOK-N1 gives
   517	
   518	\[
   519	 1=\gcd(2(u+1),u)=\gcd(2,u),
   520	\]
   521	
   522	so every multiplier is odd.  Every possible arriving full degree has
   523	
   524	\[
   525	 P_1=2C,\qquad C\ \text{odd},\qquad v_2(P_1)=1.          \tag{4.1}
   526	\]
   527	
   528	Its pole-adjacent tower vertex `X` has
   529	
   530	\[
   531	 g_X={\nu_X+1\over2\nu_X},\qquad \nu_X\ \text{odd}.     \tag{4.2}
   532	\]
   533	
   534	Thus `5/8 > g_X` exactly for the advertised odd `nu_X>=5` family.
   535	
   536	### 4.2 The off-axis resonance
   537	
   538	On the second branch the clean cell
   539	
   540	\[
   541	 (\Delta,n,\nu)=(3,2,2),\qquad d_q=5,
   542	\]
   543	
   544	sends `(w,M)=(3,2)` to `(2,1)` at price zero.  With no preceding vertex,
   545	
   546	\[
   547	 (\nu,\bar\kappa,\rho,w,P,g)=(2,5,1,2,8,5/8).           \tag{4.3}
   548	\]
   549	
   550	Both allowed arrival multiplicities `l=1,2` give the same full degree because
   551	`P'=P*d_p/l=4*(2l)/l=8`.
   552	
   553	After (4.3), `M=1,w=2`; the preceding argument forces every later
   554	characteristic multiplier to be odd.  Hence every arrival on a route
   555	containing the pole-adjacent resonance has
   556	
   557	\[
   558	 P_2=8B,\qquad B\ \text{odd},\qquad v_2(P_2)=3.          \tag{4.4}
   559	\]
   560	
   561	For completeness, zero-cost neutral padding *before* the resonance is also
   562	symbolic.  At `w=3` its characteristic `u` obeys \(3\nmid u\) by N1 and
   563	multiplies the full degree by `u`.  If `A` is the product of such prefix
   564	indices, the resonance has
   565	
   566	\[
   567	 P_2=8AB,\qquad g_R={5\over8A},\qquad v_2(P_2)\ge3,       \tag{4.5}
   568	\]
   569	
   570	where `B` is the odd post-resonance product.  Only `A=1` is the actual `5/8`
   571	intruder.  Any nonempty prefix has `P_before>=8` (every genuine chain vertex
   572	at least doubles full degree), hence `g_R<=5/16<g_X`; it is already outside
   573	the dangerous window.
   574	
   575	### 4.3 Merge rejection
   576	
   577	There is exactly one merge when `m=2`.  If it is the root, R2.1 case IV
   578	requires every arriving `w<1`; for this resonance family both branches arrive
   579	with `w=2`, so the route dies.
   580	
   581	If it is interior, current `M=1` on both branches forces
   582	`(mu_1,mu_2)=(1,1)`.  MP6(b)'s equal-quotient law, independent of the
   583	zero-slot orientation, requires
   584	
   585	\[
   586	 P_1=i_G\mu_1=i_G=P_2.                                  \tag{4.6}
   587	\]
   588	
   589	Equations (4.1) and (4.4), or the stronger (4.5), contradict (4.6) by their
   590	2-adic valuations.  Therefore the direct `5/8` family is **PROVED
   591	unrealizable at the unique merge**; E5F and the local ODE never fire.
   592	
   593	The compiler can record the finite rejection certificate
   594	
   595	```text
   596	H8_EQUAL_QUOTIENT_VP_MISMATCH {
   597	  prime: 2,
   598	  arrivals: [(mu=1, vp_pdeg=1), (mu=1, vp_pdeg>=3)]
   599	}
   600	```
   601	
   602	This removes the specific conjecture at
   603	`xmodel/sol-td11-13-scope.md:337-355`.  It does not close every other charged
   604	route of entry 11-A, and it does not prove NF-Z globally; here only the
   605	two-adic projection of the neutral scale monoid is needed, and it already
   606	separates the branches.
   607	
   608	## 5. Compiler interface
   609	
   610	The census engine should expose immutable records equivalent to the following
   611	schema.  Integers and rationals are exact; symbolic variables carry an exact
   612	domain, never an enumeration cap.
   613	
   614	```text
   615	PricedState {
   616	  version: "priced-intermerge-nf/v1"
   617	  context: ContextKey
   618	  budget: BudgetLedger
   619	  frame: Frame
   620	  producer: EntryAtom | ChainRecord | MergeRecord
   621	  endpoint: ArrivalWitness | SymbolicEndpointFamily
   622	  scale: ScaleExpr
   623	  tower: TowerHistory
   624	  merge_history: PersistentList<MergeRecord>  # equality expands parent ids
   625	  coefficient_history: CoefficientHistory
   626	  open_obligations: Set<NEEDS_NF_Z | NEEDS_NF_P | NEEDS_NF_M |
   627	                        UNRESOLVED_BUDGET_INVENTORY>
   628	}
   629	
   630	Frame {
   631	  w, M, nu, kbar, rho
   632	  Q_first_D, pdeg_full, index_i
   633	  red_dp, red_dq, X
   634	}
   635	
   636	CellAtom {
   637	  prop93_case, l, epsilon, n_pattern, n_edge, nu
   638	  k, sorted_ne_multiplicities, ell_ex
   639	  dp, dq, emitted_M
   640	  lambda_var, lambda_lb, lambda_exact_if_proved, lambda_authority
   641	  cv_inventory
   642	  factor_exponents, direct_or_pad_tag
   643	}
   644	
   645	ChainRecord { parent_state_id, cell: CellAtom }
   646	
   647	ArrivalWitness {
   648	  kind: ENTRY | DIRECT | CLEAN_RESONANCE | NEUTRAL_PAD | MERGE_EMISSION
   649	  nu, kbar, rho, M
   650	  spent_expr, path_id, producing_record_id
   651	}
   652	
   653	ScaleExpr {
   654	  exact_expression
   655	  integer_domains_and_gcd_congruences
   656	  cached_prime_valuations_and_cap_residues  # derived, checked
   657	}
   658	
   659	BudgetLedger {
   660	  gross_cap
   661	  lambda_vars[]
   662	  charge_atoms[]  # vertex id, var, lb, exact?, partition, cv inventory, authority
   663	  feasible_price_slack_inventory_constraints
   664	  spent_expr
   665	  concrete_spent_if_instantiated
   666	}
   667	
   668	TowerHistory {
   669	  pole_anchor
   670	  charged_atoms[]
   671	  neutral_summary_or_exact_word
   672	  live_factor_cap_witnesses[]
   673	  max_gap_and_witness
   674	  prefix_delta_signature
   675	  H8_scale_constraints
   676	}
   677	
   678	MergeRecord {
   679	  child_state_ids[]
   680	  arrival_mu[]
   681	  zero_slot, prop93_cases[]
   682	  family1_eta_mode: NOT_APPLICABLE | ETA_ABSORBED | ETA_FACTOR
   683	  equal_quotient_i
   684	  epsilon, nu_G, k, sorted_ne_multiplicities, ell_ex
   685	  dp, dq, kbar_G, X_G, M_G, pdeg_full_G, w_trunk
   686	  e5_offsets[]
   687	  subadditivity_authority
   688	  lambda_atom
   689	  coefficient_certificate
   690	}
   691	
   692	CoefficientHistory {
   693	  root_labelled_local_pq_certificates[]
   694	  prop81iv_verdicts_and_ideals[]
   695	  coefficient_variables_and_constraints
   696	  locality_type_if_proved
   697	}
   698	```
   699	
   700	`ScaleExpr` needs at least these constructors:
   701	
   702	```text
   703	CONST(P)
   704	CHAIN_SCALE(P, l, dp)                 # (P/l)*dp, exact integrality constraint
   705	NEUTRAL(P, u, domain)                 # P*u
   706	PURE_B(P, l, epsilon, u, domain)      # P*(l*u+epsilon)/l
   707	MERGE(i_G, dp)                         # i_G*dp
   708	PRODUCT(left, right)
   709	NEUTRAL_STAR(base, domain, summary)   # forbidden for a final census until NF-Z
   710	```
   711	
   712	The compiler rules are:
   713	
   714	1. Keep a separate state label for every distinct endpoint/path/cost/tower
   715	   signature.  Do not attach a unioned `cellmap` to `lambda_min`.
   716	2. Canonicalize a multiplicity vector by sorting only genuinely symmetric
   717	   orbit labels; never sort labelled incoming branches.
   718	3. Apply E5F to each actual or symbolic endpoint witness.  Rejection of one
   719	   witness does not reject its state until every cost-legal pad/reroute family
   720	   is exhausted.
   721	4. At every inner merge, choose `mu | current M`, enforce all
