### 8.3 FC5

FC5 is exactly

\[
w_G=\frac{\bar\kappa_G(d_q-1)}{\nu_Gd_q},
\qquad M_G=\gcd(d_p,d_q).                               \tag{8.2}
\]

It consumes an already fixed local frame and emits two scalars.  It has no
root position, exceptional divisor, transverse jet, incoming point-basis
mass, proximity relation, or cross-chart identifier.  Its noninjectivity is
visible in the banked BB2 family

\[
(\bar\kappa,d_p,d_q)=(6N+3,4N+1,2N+1),                 \tag{8.3}
\]

for which every \(N\) emits \((w,M)=(6,1)\).  See
`xmodel/sol-g5-emission.md:92-156,332-359` and
`cases/td11_census.py:1061-1120`.

Thus FC5 is useful as a scalar consistency check *after* a center certificate
exists.  It cannot produce that certificate or prove a divisibility in (6.1).

### 8.4 NF-M and the census infrastructure

NF-M bounds coefficient types only after the discrete schema and emitted
frame have been fixed; its future-locality statement requires the same type
and the same frame.  It neither names a projective point nor equates GGV root
occurrences across charts (`NF-M.md:95-122`).

The normal-form fat record and the censuses do retain useful arithmetic,
root-orbit, arrival, scale, and local coefficient information.  They do not
retain the four projective sections, the ordinary maximal ideal, exceptional
valuations, point-basis coefficients, or proximity.  Several public book keys
also deliberately aggregate different entries/contexts; the td-11 output is
an instrument-uniform death quotient, not a literal extension or occurrence
census.  Relevant audits are:

- `xmodel/sol-normalform.md:74-120,610-698`;
- `cases/book_enum.py:283-305,373-386`;
- `xmodel/sol-census-final.md:5-15,56-67`;
- `xmodel/sol-census-review.md:125-157`; and
- `TDBOUND.md:29-33`.

Accordingly, neither the 21-of-22 construction frequency nor an NF-M type
count is a center-coverage statement.  This confirms the negative conclusion
at `xmodel/sol-pcc-orbits.md:354-478`.

